WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:02.945
Now we deliberately challenge the database rules

00:00:04.691 --> 00:00:07.612
The examples test duplicate natural keys negative

00:00:07.625 --> 00:00:11.006
prices orphan foreign-key references and impossible
time

00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:15.086
ranges These are useful tests because a

00:00:15.113 --> 00:00:18.780
good design should reject them Run the

00:00:18.806 --> 00:00:21.938
provided invalid example and read the PostgreSQL

00:00:21.964 --> 00:00:24.901
error Do not treat the rejection as

00:00:24.927 --> 00:00:28.852
a failed lesson Then connect the error

00:00:28.878 --> 00:00:32.757
to the constraint responsible for it A

00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:34.612
strong schema is not only one that

00:00:34.639 --> 00:00:37.628
accepts valid data It is also one

00:00:37.655 --> 00:00:42.397
that reliably refuses invalid data When designing

00:00:42.423 --> 00:00:44.344
your own tables plan at least one

00:00:44.357 --> 00:00:47.873
invalid-data test for each important rule That

00:00:47.899 --> 00:00:50.320
helps confirm the guardrail actually works
